  "atoms": [
    {
      "id": "a0",
      "text": "Urbain Le Verrier reported an anomalous advance of Mercury's perihelion in 1859.",
      "label": "p1",
      "factual": true,
      "role": "claim",
      "pos": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "a1",
      "text": "Le Verrier derived the anomaly from an analysis of recorded transits of Mercury across the solar disk.",
      "label": "p2",
      "factual": true,
      "role": "claim",
      "pos": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "a2",
      "text": "The unexplained advance of Mercury's perihelion amounts to about 43 arcseconds per century.",
      "label": "p3",
      "factual": true,
      "role": "claim",
      "pos": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "a3",
      "text": "Mercury's perihelion drifts forward by roughly 43 seconds of arc each hundred years beyond the Newtonian prediction.",
      "label": "p4",
      "factual": true,
      "role": "claim",
      "pos": 4
    },
    {
      "id": "a4",
      "text": "Le Verrier proposed an unseen intramercurial planet, later named Vulcan, as the cause of the residual motion.",
      "label": "p5",
      "factual": true,
      "role": "claim",
      "pos": 5
    },
    {
      "id": "a5",
      "text": "Le Verrier accounted for the anomaly by positing an additional moon in orbit around Mercury.",
      "label": "p6",
      "factual": false,
      "role": "claim",
      "pos": 6
    },
    {
      "id": "a6",
      "text": "Edmond Lescarbault reported seeing a dark round object cross the face of the Sun in 1859.",
      "label": "p7",
      "factual": true,
      "role": "claim",
      "pos": 7
    },
    {
      "id": "a7",
      "text": "Astronomers mounted searches for an intramercurial planet during total solar eclipses in the 1870s and 1880s.",
      "label": "p8",
      "factual": true,
      "role": "claim",
      "pos": 8
    },
    {
      "id": "a8",
      "text": "No intramercurial planet has ever been confirmed to exist.",
      "label": "p9",
      "factual": true,
      "role": "claim",
      "pos": 9,
      "factual_note": "exhaustive_pair: exactly one of this pair is false"
    },
    {
      "id": "a9",
      "text": "A planet orbiting between Mercury and the Sun was confirmed by later observations.",
      "label": "p10",
      "factual": true,
      "role": "claim",
      "pos": 10,
      "factual_note": "exhaustive_pair: exactly one of this pair is false"
    },
    {
      "id": "a10",
      "text": "A subsequent assessment of the eclipse search records determined that the reported intramercurial objects were known stars.",
      "label": "p11",
      "factual": true,
      "role": "claim",
      "pos": 11
    },
    {
      "id": "a11",
      "text": "Hugo von Seeliger proposed that a distribution of zodiacal dust inside Mercury's orbit produced the residual advance.",
      "label": "p12",
      "factual": true,
      "role": "claim",
      "pos": 12
    },
    {
      "id": "a12",
      "text": "No ring of zodiacal dust of the density required to shift Mercury's perihelion lies inside Mercury's orbit.",
      "label": "p13",
      "factual": true,
      "role": "claim",
      "pos": 13
    },
    {
      "id": "a13",
      "text": "The Sun's quadrupole moment is far too small to generate the observed residual advance.",
      "label": "p14",
      "factual": true,
      "role": "claim",
      "pos": 14
    },
    {
      "id": "a14",
      "text": "Einstein presented a general-relativistic calculation of Mercury's perihelion advance in November 1915.",
      "label": "p15",
      "factual": true,
      "role": "claim",
      "pos": 15
    },
    {
      "id": "a15",
      "text": "Einstein published his treatment of Mercury's perihelion in his 1905 paper on special relativity.",
      "label": "p16",
      "factual": false,
      "role": "claim",
      "pos": 16
    }
